WEBVTT

NOTE SA1 Topic 5.3: DHCP Failover and Troubleshooting. Timings are estimates at 150 words a minute; re-sync after recording.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Welcome to Topic 5.

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:03.850
3, DHCP Failover and Troubleshooting.

3
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.300
In Lab 5.

4
00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:08.350
2 you built one DHCP server, SRV01.

5
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:10.600
That works, until SRV01 stops.

6
00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:17.650
In this topic you will see why one DHCP
server is a single point of failure, and

7
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:21.500
how the old split scope compares with DHCP
failover.

8
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:26.950
You will choose a failover mode and set
the maximum client lead time.

9
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.400
You will configure failover in the wizard
and in PowerShell, and replicate changes

10
00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:33.850
to the partner.

11
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.900
Then you troubleshoot clients without an
address.

12
00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:39.550
A lease is borrowed, not owned.

13
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.600
With the 8-day lease from Lab 5.

14
00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:47.650
2, CLIENT01 tries to renew with SRV01 at
half time, day 4.

15
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.700
If SRV01 does not answer, at 87.

16
00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:54.550
5 percent, day 7, it asks any DHCP server.

17
00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:01.600
If SRV01 is down for an hour, clients with
a lease hardly notice, but a new client

18
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.050
gets nothing.

19
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:09.700
If SRV01 is down for days, leases run out,
and clients fall back to a 169.

20
00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:11.150
254 address.

21
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.600
The fix is a second server that knows the
same scope and leases.

22
00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:19.250
The old fix was split scope.

23
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:26.300
Two servers each held part of the range,
often 80 percent on one and 20 on the

24
00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.550
other, with the rest excluded.

25
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:35.200
But the servers shared no lease data, the
small part could run out, and you set

26
00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:37.050
options and reservations twice.

27
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.700
The current way is DHCP failover.

28
00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:45.150
Both partners hold the whole scope and
copy every lease to each other.

29
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:50.600
It works for IPv4 scopes only, with
exactly two servers in a relationship.

30
00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:54.050
A relationship is the named link between
them.

31
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:55.900
Failover has two modes.

32
00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:58.150
Load balance is the default.

33
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:04.400
Both servers answer at the same time, and
a hash of each client's MAC address

34
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.250
decides which one answers.

35
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:11.700
The split is 50 50 by default, and you can
choose any percentage.

36
00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:16.750
In hot standby, the active server serves
everyone and the standby waits.

37
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:23.000
It keeps a reserve of addresses, 5 percent
by default, for new clients during an

38
00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.450
outage.

39
00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.900
This fits a backup server at another site.

40
00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:29.350
Lab 5.

41
00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.000
3 uses load balance, 50 50.

42
00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:33.450
Three more settings.

43
00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:40.100
Maximum Client Lead Time, or MCLT, limits
how far a server may go beyond what its

44
00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:41.550
partner knows.

45
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:45.800
Without its partner, it gives only short
leases, MCLT long.

46
00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.050
The default is one hour.

47
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.500
The state switchover interval is off by
default.

48
00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:58.150
Turn it on, and a server that lost its
partner declares it down by itself after

49
00:02:58.400 --> 00:02:59.600
the interval.

50
00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:04.650
The shared secret turns on message
authentication, so a stranger cannot pose

51
00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:06.100
as a partner.

52
00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:09.550
Choose your own secret, never the pod
password.

53
00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.800
Each partner has a state.

54
00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:16.050
In Normal, the partners talk, and each
serves its share.

55
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:20.300
When contact is lost, a server goes to
Communication Interrupted.

56
00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:26.550
It cannot tell whether its partner crashed
or only the network broke, In load balance

57
00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:30.000
it then answers all clients, even its
partner's.

58
00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:36.650
It renews their leases for the MCLT only,
and gives new leases from its own free

59
00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:38.100
pool first.

60
00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:42.750
Partner Down is set by you, or by the
switchover timer.

61
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.000
After the MCLT, that server takes over the
whole scope.

62
00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:53.250
When contact returns, the partners compare
leases and go back to Normal on their own.

63
00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:59.100
In the DHCP console on SRV01, right-click
IPv4, or the scope, and choose Configure

64
00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:00.550
Failover.

65
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.600
On the first page, clear Select all and
tick the scope 192.

66
00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:07.050
168.

67
00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:08.500
50.

68
00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:09.950
0.

69
00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.400
Then type the partner server, SRV-CORE at
192.

70
00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:14.850
168.

71
00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.300
50.

72
00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:17.750
21.

73
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.800
The partner must already run DHCP and be
authorized in Active Directory.

74
00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:28.250
Clear the box that says Reuse existing
failover relationships, because you want a

75
00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:29.700
new one.

76
00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:35.950
Then select Next to reach the most
important page of the wizard, the one that

77
00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:37.400
creates the relationship.

78
00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:42.050
On Create a new failover relationship,
type the Relationship Name, SRV01-SRVCORE.

79
00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:45.100
It must be unique on both partners.

80
00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:48.950
Leave the Maximum Client Lead Time at 1
hour.

81
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:55.600
Mode is Load balance, with 50 percent for
the local server and 50 for the partner.

82
00:04:55.850 --> 00:05:01.050
Leave State Switchover Interval cleared,
so you decide when a partner is really

83
00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:02.500
down.

84
00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:07.950
Keep Enable Message Authentication on and
type your shared secret; it shows as

85
00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:09.400
stars.

86
00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:13.650
Then Next, Finish, and check that every
task says Successful.

87
00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:17.500
Here is the same job in PowerShell on
SRV01.

88
00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:22.150
Read-Host with AsSecureString asks for the
secret at a hidden prompt.

89
00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:27.600
The SharedSecret parameter wants plain
text, so the next line converts it in

90
00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:29.050
memory only.

91
00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:33.700
Then Add-DhcpServerv4Failover: the name,
the partner, the scope, 50 percent, an

92
00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:36.750
MCLT of one hour, and the secret.

93
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.000
Dash Force skips a prompt.

94
00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:41.250
Remove-Variable clears the plain copy.

95
00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:45.100
Get-DhcpServerv4Failover then shows the
relationship, LoadBalance, 50, one hour,

96
00:05:45.350 --> 00:05:46.950
and the state Normal.

97
00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.200
Run it on SRV-CORE too.

98
00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:52.650
Here is a rule that surprises many people.

99
00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:56.900
Leases copy themselves between partners,
but scope settings do not.

100
00:05:57.150 --> 00:06:03.150
If you change an option, a reservation, an
exclusion or the lease time on SRV01,

101
00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:06.600
SRV-CORE keeps the old value until you
replicate.

102
00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:11.650
Right-click the scope and choose Replicate
Scope, or Replicate Relationship for every

103
00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:13.100
scope in it.

104
00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:14.950
In PowerShell, use
Invoke-DhcpServerv4FailoverReplication.

105
00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:17.200
Careful: replication overwrites the
partner.

106
00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:24.250
Always start it on the server that has the
settings you want to keep, and make your

107
00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:26.900
changes on that one server only.

108
00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:28.350
Now troubleshooting.

109
00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:31.800
A good Server+ habit: start at the client.

110
00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:34.450
On CLIENT01, ipconfig release and renew.

111
00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:40.300
If renew says it is unable to contact your
DHCP server, no server answered.

112
00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:42.150
Look at ipconfig all.

113
00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.400
An Autoconfiguration address in 169.

114
00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:46.250
254, with mask 255.

115
00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:47.700
255.

116
00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:49.150
0.

117
00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:52.600
0, is APIPA: Windows picked its own
address.

118
00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:55.650
It reaches no server and no DNS.

119
00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:59.100
So APIPA is a clue, not a fix.

120
00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:05.750
When the problem is solved, renew again,
and you see a real address and the DHCP

121
00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.600
server that gave it.

122
00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:09.850
Then move toward the server.

123
00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:12.100
If every client gets 169.

124
00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:15.950
254, check whether the DHCP Server service
is stopped.

125
00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:21.400
If the service runs but never answers,
check authorization: in a domain, a

126
00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:26.850
Windows DHCP server must be listed in
Active Directory before it serves anyone.

127
00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:28.700
That stops rogue servers.

128
00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:34.550
If old clients are fine and new ones fail,
the scope may be full.

129
00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:40.000
If the address is fine but names fail,
check options 6 and 15.

130
00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:45.450
And if only another subnet fails, the
router may lack a relay agent.

131
00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:48.500
Three cmdlets cover most of that table.

132
00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:53.150
Get-Service DHCPServer shows whether the
service is Running; if not, Start-Service.

133
00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:55.800
Get-DhcpServerInDC lists the authorized
DHCP servers.

134
00:07:56.050 --> 00:08:00.050
In the lab you should see both SRV01 and
SRV-CORE.

135
00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:03.500
If one is missing, authorize it with
Add-DhcpServerInDC.

136
00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:07.350
Get-DhcpServerv4ScopeStatistics shows free
and used addresses for each scope.

137
00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:11.600
Watch the PercentageInUse column, and run
it on both partners.

138
00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:17.850
Near 100 means a full scope: widen the
range or shorten the lease time so

139
00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:19.700
addresses come back sooner.

140
00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:25.150
A new client has no address, so it shouts:
it sends a broadcast.

141
00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:27.400
Routers do not forward broadcasts.

142
00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:29.650
So a client on 192.

143
00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:31.100
168.

144
00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:32.550
60.

145
00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:36.000
0 never reaches a DHCP server on 192.

146
00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:37.450
168.

147
00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:38.900
50.

148
00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:41.950
0, even when the right scope exists.

149
00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:48.600
The answer is a DHCP relay agent on that
subnet, often called IP helper on routers.

150
00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:52.850
It catches the broadcast and forwards it
to the servers.

151
00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:57.900
It adds its own address, so the server
picks the matching scope.

152
00:08:58.150 --> 00:09:00.950
With failover, point it at both partners.

153
00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:05.200
Our pod has one subnet, so this is a
concept.

154
00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:11.450
Every DHCP server also keeps a plain text
audit log in C colon backslash Windows,

155
00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:16.100
System32, dhcp, one file for each weekday,
like DhcpSrvLog-Mon dot log.

156
00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:19.550
Read the end with Get-Content and dash
Tail.

157
00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:25.800
Each line starts with an event ID, then
the date, time, a description, the IP

158
00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:28.450
address, host name and MAC address.

159
00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:33.500
ID 10 is a new lease, 11 a renewal, 12 a
release.

160
00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:36.550
ID 14 means the scope was full.

161
00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:40.800
IDs 54 and 56 mean the server was not
authorized.

162
00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:42.650
Four mistakes to avoid.

163
00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:46.900
One: changing an option or reservation on
one partner only.

164
00:09:47.150 --> 00:09:50.350
Replicate from the server with the right
settings.

165
00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:54.200
Two: expecting failover for DHCPv6 or for
three servers.

166
00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:57.650
Failover is IPv4 only, two servers per
relationship.

167
00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:03.100
Three: typing the shared secret into a
script, or reusing the pod password.

168
00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:08.950
Choose your own and type it at a hidden
prompt or in the wizard.

169
00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:12.800
Four: installing the second server but
never authorizing it.

170
00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:15.050
Check that Get-DhcpServerInDC lists both.

171
00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:16.900
Time to check yourself.

172
00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:19.950
SRV01 and SRV-CORE share the scope 192.

173
00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:21.400
168.

174
00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:22.850
50.

175
00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:24.300
0.

176
00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:26.150
First, CLIENT01 shows 169.

177
00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:27.600
254.

178
00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:29.050
23.

179
00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:30.500
7.

180
00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:35.150
What does that tell you, and where do you
look first?

181
00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:38.200
Second, you change option 6 on SRV01.

182
00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:42.050
Why can a client still get the old value?

183
00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:45.500
Third, SRV-CORE moves to a small backup
site.

184
00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:47.750
Which mode fits better now?

185
00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:49.600
Fourth, clients on 192.

186
00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:51.050
168.

187
00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:52.500
60.

188
00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:57.150
0 behind a router get no address, but the
scope exists.

189
00:10:57.400 --> 00:10:58.600
What is missing?

190
00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:02.050
Pause the video and answer all four
questions.

191
00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:03.900
Here are the answers.

192
00:11:04.150 --> 00:11:05.350
One: 169.

193
00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:08.800
254 is APIPA, so no DHCP server answered.

194
00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:14.250
Check the client's network, then the DHCP
Server service on both partners, then

195
00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:15.700
authorization.

196
00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:21.550
Two: scope settings do not replicate by
themselves, so SRV-CORE still has the old

197
00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:23.000
option.

198
00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:25.250
Replicate the scope from SRV01.

199
00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:26.700
Three: hot standby.

200
00:11:26.950 --> 00:11:32.550
The local server serves everyone, and the
remote partner keeps its 5 percent reserve

201
00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:34.000
for an outage.

202
00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:39.450
Four: a relay agent on the router, because
broadcasts stop at the router.

203
00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:42.100
Let's watch it in the pod.

204
00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:48.350
On SRV01 I open the DHCP console and run
Configure Failover for our scope, with

205
00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:54.200
SRV-CORE as partner, load balance 50 50,
and a secret I type off camera.

206
00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:58.850
On SRV-CORE the scope and the CLIENT01
reservation are already there.

207
00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:02.300
Now I change the lease time on SRV01.

208
00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:06.550
SRV-CORE still shows 8 days, until I
choose Replicate Scope.

209
00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:09.200
Next I stop DHCP on SRV01.

210
00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:11.050
CLIENT01 still gets 192.

211
00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:12.500
168.

212
00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:13.950
50.

213
00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:15.800
150, now from SRV-CORE.

214
00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:20.450
I stop SRV-CORE too, and CLIENT01 falls
back to a 169.

215
00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:21.900
254 address.

216
00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:23.350
To wrap up.

217
00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:28.000
Failover replaces split scope: two
servers, one IPv4 scope, shared leases.

218
00:12:28.250 --> 00:12:33.450
Load balance shares the clients; hot
standby waits with a 5 percent reserve.

219
00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:38.900
Keep MCLT at one hour, switchover off, and
use your own shared secret.

220
00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:42.750
Replicate scope changes by hand, from the
right server.

221
00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:45.400
And when a client shows 169.

222
00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:50.450
254, work from the client to the server:
service, authorization, scope, relay.

223
00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:51.900
In Lab 5.

224
00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:56.550
3, about 90 minutes and practice only, you
rebuild Lab 5.

225
00:12:56.800 --> 00:13:00.400
2, add SRV-CORE, create the relationship,
and test it.
